## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-input serving with optional inputs and multiple signatures in TF SavedModel.

## Key Concepts
- tf.saved_model, serving signatures, tf.keras.Input, tf.function, concrete functions, multiple signatures, default inputs.

## Code Example
```javascript
import tensorflow as tf

# Python-like example shown here for TF SavedModel export

def build_model():
    image = tf.keras.Input(shape=(224,224,3), name='image')
    feats = tf.keras.Input(shape=(10,), name='features')
    x = tf.keras.layers.Flatten()(image)
    x = tf.keras.layers.Concatenate()([x, feats])
    out = tf.keras.layers.Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')(x)
    return tf.keras.Model(inputs=[image, feats], outputs=out)

model = build_model()

@tf.function(input_signature=[tf.TensorSpec([None,224,224,3], tf.float32, name='image'),
                              tf.TensorSpec([None,10], tf.float32, name='features')])
def serve_full(image, features):
    return {'outputs': model([image, features])}

@tf.function(input_signature=[tf.TensorSpec([None,224,224,3], tf.float32, name='image')])
def serve_image_only(image):
    default_features = tf.zeros([tf.shape(image)[0], 10], dtype=tf.float32)
    return {'outputs': model([image, default_features])}

tf.saved_model.save(model, 'saved_model', signatures={
  'serving_default': serve_full.get_concrete_function(),
  'image_only': serve_image_only.get_concrete_function(),
})
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you version and test signature compatibility across model upgrades?
- How to monitor and fallback if image_only signature is called with missing features?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-model optimizations that preserve SLA and isolate tail latency in a multi-model serving setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-model caching for shared embeddings
- Per-model batching with HoLB isolation
- Cache sizing and eviction strategy
- Profiling and latency targeting

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo JS-like sketch; real impl would be in Python with TF Serving hooks
class LRUCache {
  constructor(cap) { this.cap = cap; this.map = new Map(); }
  get(k) { if (!this.map.has(k)) return null; const v = this.map.get(k); this.map.delete(k); this.map.set(k, v); return v; }
  put(k, v) { this.map.set(k, v); if (this.map.size > this.cap) { const first = this.map.keys().next().value; this.map.delete(first); } }
}
```
```python
from collections import OrderedDict
class LRUCache:
    def __init__(self, cap):
        self.cap = cap
        self.store = OrderedDict()
    def get(self, k):
        if k not in self.store: return None
        self.store.move_to_end(k)
        return self.store[k]
    def put(self, k, v):
        self.store[k] = v
        self.store.move_to_end(k)
        if len(self.store) > self.cap:
            self.store.popitem(last=False)
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure cache hit rate vs. latency impact?
- How would you handle cache coherence during model updates and embeddings drift?
